# Terminates TLS for both public-facing services — §9.2's own diagram: a
# single Caddy edge, everything else reachable only on the compose
# network. Two domains, not one domain with path-based routing: sms-gateway
# is an OIDC issuer, and `SMS_OIDC_ISSUER` must be its own https origin —
# `/.well-known/openid-configuration` and `/jwks.json` are only
# well-defined at an origin's root (RFC 8414), and `authkestra_op`'s
# discovery handler builds absolute URLs off the configured issuer, not off
# whatever path Caddy happened to route through. A shared domain would need
# Caddy to rewrite those URLs in the response body, which is not something
# a reverse proxy does for you.
#
# Both domain vars are required (deploy/docker-compose.yml's own
# `${SMS_GATEWAY_DOMAIN:?}` / `${SMS_ADMIN_DOMAIN:?}` fail the whole stack
# rather than start half-configured) — set them in deploy/.env.
#
# Real deploy, real DNS pointed at this VM: nothing further needed below.
# Caddy's automatic HTTPS (ACME via Let's Encrypt, using CADDY_ACME_EMAIL)
# takes over on its own the moment it can complete an HTTP-01 or TLS-ALPN
# challenge against a real hostname.
#
# Local/demo run with no public DNS: swap the commented `tls internal`
# line in for the real cert flow in both blocks below — Caddy then mints
# its own local CA and self-signed certs. See
# docs/runbooks/deployment.md for trusting that CA on your own machine.
#
# #156 — rate limiting `/token` and `/dlr/{providerKey}` at this edge.
# `docs/architecture.md` §4.2/§4.6 assign this duty to "the reverse proxy",
# not `sms-auth` — #22 closed the Argon2-amplification version of this
# concern on that reasoning, and #139 then built this Caddy edge without
# ever picking the duty up (`grep rate_limit deploy/Caddyfile` found
# nothing before this change). `rate_limit` is a third-party Caddy module
# (github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit) — stock `caddy:2-alpine` does not
# have it (confirmed: `caddy version`/`caddy list-modules` in that image
# has no `http.handlers.rate_limit`). This deploy tree now builds its own
# image from `deploy/caddy.Dockerfile` instead — see that file's own
# header for exactly what changes and why, and this PR's description for
# the `deploy/docker-compose.yml` edit it requires (not made here — #156
# scopes this agent to this file, `deploy/.env.example`, and
# `docs/runbooks/deployment.md` only).
#
# Every numeric limit below is env-tunable with a documented default (see
# `deploy/.env.example`'s own "Caddy rate limiting" section for the
# reasoning behind each number) so an operator can retune without a
# rebuild — only the *keys* (what a limiter buckets requests by) are
# hardcoded, since getting those wrong is a security property, not a
# capacity tuning knob.
#
# **What "client_id and source IP" from §4.2 actually reduces to here,
# and why — read before changing the keys below.** §4.2 requires `/token`
# be keyed on client_id *and* source IP, not client_id alone, because a
# client_id is not secret (it is a public identifier — every worked
# example in `examples/` prints it) and a global-per-client_id bucket
# would let an attacker who merely knows a legitimate client_id exhaust
# it and deny that real client service. This edge cannot key on client_id
# at all, for a real, checked-not-assumed reason: `client_id` on `/token`
# arrives only in the `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` POST body (see
# `packages/gateway/src/token.ts`'s and `examples/rust/sms-send`'s own
# request construction — no header, no query string, no Basic auth carries
# it under `private_key_jwt`), and every way this edge could read a
# specific form field out of that body was checked and rejected:
#   - Core Caddy's own `{http.request.body}` placeholder exists, but
#     `modules/caddyhttp/app.go` documents it "⚠️ inefficient; use only
#     for debugging" — and reading it risks not leaving `reverse_proxy`
#     an intact body to forward, which would break every real `/token`
#     exchange, not just the attacker's.
#   - `RussellLuo/caddy-ext/requestbodyvar` (the one third-party module
#     built for exactly this) only parses `application/json` and `*/xml`
#     (read from its own `querier.go`) — form-urlencoded is not one of
#     the two, and hits `unsupported Media Type`.
#   - The one third-party module found that does parse form bodies,
#     `erfianugrah/caddy-body-matcher`, is a single-contributor repo with
#     0 stars and 0 forks, created within the last few months (checked
#     against the GitHub API, not assumed) — an unacceptable supply-chain
#     bar for code parsing OAuth request bodies on a TLS-terminating,
#     internet-facing production edge.
# So this edge keys `/token` on source IP only (`token_per_ip` below),
# which already satisfies the specific attack #156's own acceptance
# criteria names — "a single abusive caller must not be able to deny
# service to a legitimate client sharing its client_id" — an attacker at
# one IP can only ever exhaust *that IP's own* bucket; a legitimate
# client at a different IP, sharing the same client_id, is unaffected,
# because there is no shared client_id-keyed bucket to exhaust. What
# per-IP-only keying does *not* give you is §4.2's other half: an
# attacker sharing a legitimate client's *source IP* (realistic behind a
# NAT gateway or shared egress, not for a dedicated service-account
# caller) could still exhaust that shared IP bucket. Tracked as a real,
# not cosmetic, gap — filed as #168. **Closed, not by this file**:
# `app/sms-gateway/src/token_rate_limit.rs` is a second, defense-in-depth
# limiter mounted on `/token` itself (in `app/sms-gateway/src/op.rs`,
# which already owns route assembly for this path — not `sms-auth`, and
# not a first-party Caddy module, both weighed and rejected in #168's own
# issue), keyed on the real `client_id` it reads from the already-buffered
# body. This zone's own per-IP keying stays exactly as it is — the two
# layers are complementary, not redundant: this one bounds abuse by
# source IP before it ever reaches the application; that one bounds abuse
# by claimed identity, the dimension this file structurally cannot reach.
# `token_global` below is a
# third mitigation in the meantime — a single aggregate budget across
# *all* callers, which bounds the worst case (many IPs, one or many
# claimed client_ids, all hammering `/token` at once) even though it
# can't attribute blame to one caller the way a real composite key would.
#
# `/dlr/{providerKey}` does not have this problem: `providerKey` is a URL
# *path* segment (`app/sms-gateway/src/dlr.rs` mounts
# `POST /dlr/{providerKey}`), not a body field, so it is one of Caddy's
# most ordinary placeholders (`{http.request.uri.path}`) — no body
# parsing, no third-party risk. `dlr_per_source` below keys on IP *and*
# path together, which is the real "client_id and source IP" shape §4.2
# asks for, just spelled with the identifier this route actually carries
# in the clear. It is also worth rate limiting for the same reason as
# `/token`: also unauthenticated by design (a provider webhook carries no
# bearer token — #34's own module doc), and also a Postgres write on
# every call (`dlr.rs` matches against `Message` and writes a
# `DeliveryReceipt` per request).
#
# Neither zone below trusts `X-Forwarded-For`/`client_ip` — deliberately:
# `deploy/docker-compose.yml` publishes Caddy's own ports directly to the
# host (`grep -n 'ports:' -A3 deploy/docker-compose.yml`), there is no
# upstream load balancer or CDN in front of it in this topology, so
# `{http.request.remote.host}` (the actual TCP peer address Caddy
# accepted the connection from) is the real client IP and cannot be
# spoofed by a request header. If this deployment ever grows a real
# upstream proxy (a cloud LB, a CDN), these zones need `trusted_proxies`
# configured globally and their keys switched to a placeholder that
# honors it — keying on `remote.host` unchanged in that topology would
# silently bucket every request behind the LB into one IP, collapsing
# per-client isolation rather than merely losing it loudly.
#
# `ipv6_prefix 64` on both per-source zones: caddy-ratelimit's own README
# names the exact attack this defends — an attacker with a routed IPv6
# `/64` can cycle through billions of individual addresses to dodge a
# naive per-address bucket; masking to `/64` buckets the whole prefix
# together instead. No IPv4 equivalent is set — an attacker would need to
# actually control a `/N` block of IPv4 space to get the same effect,
# meaningfully rarer.
#
# Single Caddy instance in this compose stack (`deploy/docker-compose.yml`
# runs exactly one `caddy` service, no replicas) — caddy-ratelimit's
# in-memory state is authoritative as-is. A `distributed { }` block only
# matters the moment a second Caddy replica exists; add it then, not
# preemptively against a topology this stack doesn't have.

{
	email {$CADDY_ACME_EMAIL}
}

{$SMS_GATEWAY_DOMAIN} {
	# tls internal

	rate_limit {
		# Per-IP budget on /token. Sized against real caller behaviour,
		# not a round number: `packages/gateway/src/token.ts` and
		# `examples/rust/sms-send` both cache the access token until
		# `exp - 60s` off a 15-minute default TTL, i.e. one legitimate
		# `/token` call roughly every 14 minutes per running client
		# process under steady state. 20 events/minute is >20x that
		# steady-state rate — generous enough that a fleet restart (every
		# instance of one client re-minting a token at once) or a burst
		# of retries after a transient failure never gets throttled,
		# while still capping a single-IP flood to a small, fixed
		# multiple of real usage instead of an unbounded rate hammering
		# `authkestra-op`'s RS256 verification and the `ClientAssertion`
		# replay check on every request.
		zone token_per_ip {
			match {
				path /token
			}
			key {http.request.remote.host}
			events {$RATE_LIMIT_TOKEN_PER_IP_EVENTS:20}
			window {$RATE_LIMIT_TOKEN_PER_IP_WINDOW:1m}
			ipv6_prefix 64
		}

		# Aggregate budget across every caller — the mitigation named
		# above for the gap per-IP-only keying leaves open (many source
		# IPs, one or many claimed client_ids, all hammering `/token`
		# at once). A static key means exactly one bucket for the whole
		# zone. 120 events/minute mirrors `docs/architecture.md` §4.5's
		# own worked `RateLimitLayer` example (`RateLimitConfig::new(120,
		# 2.0)`, burst 120) for the authenticated surface — reusing a
		# number this codebase already reasoned about once, rather than
		# inventing an unrelated one for the same order-of-magnitude
		# concern.
		zone token_global {
			match {
				path /token
			}
			key token-aggregate
			events {$RATE_LIMIT_TOKEN_GLOBAL_EVENTS:120}
			window {$RATE_LIMIT_TOKEN_GLOBAL_WINDOW:1m}
		}

		# Per-(IP, providerKey) budget on DLR callbacks. Sized against
		# §4.6's own documented constraint, not a round number: the
		# worker's `dispatch` role respects "Orange's hard 5 TPS cap" on
		# *outbound* submissions, so real inbound DLR traffic for one
		# provider is bounded by a small multiple of 5/s (a message can
		# receive more than one status callback — e.g. an intermediate
		# then a final state). 10 events/second leaves 2x headroom above
		# that 5 TPS ceiling before throttling engages.
		#
		# Keyed on the bare source IP, NOT the composite
		# `{remote.host}_{uri.path}` an earlier revision used. That
		# composite silently disabled `ipv6_prefix`: the module's
		# `applyNetworkPrefix` does `ip := net.ParseIP(key); if ip == nil
		# { return key }`, and `2001:db8::1_/dlr/orange_cm` never parses
		# as an IP, so masking was skipped and an IPv6 attacker could
		# rotate addresses inside a /64 to mint a fresh bucket per
		# request. Verified against the module source, not its docs.
		#
		# The cost is losing per-providerKey isolation, which buys
		# nothing today: `orange_cm` is the only provider wired up
		# (AGENTS.md), so the path dimension has exactly one value. When
		# a second provider lands (#63), add a second zone rather than
		# reinstating a composite key here — the IPv6 masking is the
		# security property, the path split is only fairness.
		zone dlr_per_source {
			match {
				path /dlr/*
			}
			key {http.request.remote.host}
			events {$RATE_LIMIT_DLR_PER_KEY_EVENTS:100}
			window {$RATE_LIMIT_DLR_PER_KEY_WINDOW:10s}
			ipv6_prefix 64
		}

		# Aggregate DLR budget, same defense-in-depth reasoning as
		# token_global — bounds total Postgres write load from this route
		# regardless of how many distinct (IP, providerKey) buckets an
		# attacker spreads across. 4x the per-source ceiling: generous
		# enough that legitimate concurrent traffic across every
		# configured provider never trips it, since only one provider
		# (`orange_cm`) is wired up in this milestone (AGENTS.md).
		zone dlr_global {
			match {
				path /dlr/*
			}
			key dlr-aggregate
			events {$RATE_LIMIT_DLR_GLOBAL_EVENTS:200}
			window {$RATE_LIMIT_DLR_GLOBAL_WINDOW:10s}
		}

		log_key
	}

	# #70/#71: `/metrics` deliberately does not need a zone here. It never
	# reaches this `reverse_proxy` at all — sms-gateway's own `/metrics`
	# listener is a second, separate port (9090, default
	# SMS_METRICS_LISTEN_ADDR) this block never names, not a route on
	# 8080 — so there is nothing below for an attacker to even request
	# through this domain. See crates/sms-api/src/metrics.rs's own module
	# doc for the full reasoning (the same "must not become a DoS
	# amplifier"/reconnaissance-value question `/readyz`'s R1 exception
	# already answers for a different route).
	reverse_proxy sms-gateway:8080
}

{$SMS_ADMIN_DOMAIN} {
	# tls internal
	reverse_proxy admin:8080
}
